pen({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06288v1\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 538\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†1 Introduction L16:   2. cite6†2 Background L17:     1. cite7†2.1 LLM Inference Optimization L18:     2. cite8†2.2 Aggregated vs. Disaggregated Serving L19:   3. cite9†3 Motivation L20:   4. cite10†4 Design and Implementation L21:     1. cite11†4.1 Workflow of AIConfigurator L22:     2. cite12†4.2 Performance Modeling L23:       1. cite13†4.2.1 Static Mode L24:       2. cite14†4.2.2 Aggregated Mode L25:       3. cite15†4.2.3 Disaggregated Mode L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Iteration-level Modeling L27:       1. cite17†4.3.1 Decompose Iteration Into Operators L28:     4. cite18†4.4 Operator Database L29:       1. cite19†4.4.1 Power Law Correction for MoE L30:   5. cite20†5 Evaluation L31:     1. cite21†5.1 Aggregated Serving Fidelity L32:     2. cite22†5.2 Disaggregated Serving Fidelity L33:     3. cite23†5.3 Search Efficiency L34:     4. cite24†5.4 Case Study: Finding the Optimal L35:   6. cite25†6 Related Work L36:   7. cite26†7 Conclusion L37:   8. cite27†References L38: cite28†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L39: \nL40: arXiv:2601.06288v1 [cs.LG] 09 Jan 2026\nL41: # AIConfigurator: Lightning-Fast Configuration Optimization for Multi-Framework LLM Serving\nL42: \nL43: Tianhao Xu    Yiming Liu    Xianglong Lu    Yijia Zhao    Xuting Zhou    Aichen Feng    Yiyi Chen Affiliation: Yi Shen    Qin Zhou    Xumeng Chen    Ilya Sherstyuk    Haorui Li    Rishi Thakkar    Ben Hamm Affiliation: Yuanzhe Li    Xue Huang    Wenpeng Wu    Anish Shanbhag    Harry Kim    Chuan Chen    Junjie Lai Affiliation: NVIDIA\nL44: ## Abstract\nL45: Optimizing Large Language Model (LLM) inference in production systems is increasingly difficult due to dynamic workloads, stringent latency/throughput targets, and a rapidly expanding configuration space. This complexity spans not only distributed parallelism strategies (tensor/pipeline/expert) but also intricate framework-specific runtime parameters such as those concerning the enablement of CUDA graphs, available KV-cache memory fractions, and maximum token capacity, which drastically impact performance.\nL46: The diversity of modern inference frameworks (e.g., TRT-LLM, vLLM, SGLang), each employing distinct kernels and execution policies, makes manual tuning both framework-specific and computationally prohibitive. We present AIConfigurator, a unified performance-modeling system that enables rapid, framework-agnostic inference configuration search without requiring GPU-based profiling.\nL47: AIConfigurator combines (1) a methodology that decomposes inference into analytically modelable primitives—GEMM, attention, communication, and memory operations while capturing framework-specific scheduling dynamics; (2) a calibrated kernel-level performance database for these primitives across a wide range of hardware platforms and popular open-weights models (GPT-OSS, Qwen, DeepSeek, LLama, Mistral); and (3) an abstraction layer that automatically resolves optimal launch parameters for the target backend, seamlessly integrating into production-grade orchestration systems.\nL48: Evaluation on production LLM serving workloads demonstrates that AIConfigurator identifies superior serving configurations that improve performance by up to 40% for dense models (e.g., Qwen3-32B) and 50% for MoE architectures (e.g., DeepSeek-V3), while completing searches within 30 seconds on average, enabling the rapid exploration of vast design spaces—from cluster topology down to engine specific flags.\nL49: ## 1 Introduction\nL50: The rapid evolution of Large Language Models (LLMs) has placed unprecedented demands on computational infrastructure. As state-of-the-art parameter counts scale from hundreds of millions to hundreds of billions, the efficiency of inference deployment has become a critical determinant of economic viability. However, optimizing these deployments is fraught with complexity.\n"
